<Project>
  <!--
    The single reviewed source of the CheatEngine.SDK package this Client consumes (audit ADR-10, A21-01).

    Directory.Packages.props imports this file, so every project sees these values before its own body, including the
    Coexistence fixtures that turn Central Package Management off. Every MSBuild usage derives from them:
    - the central PackageVersion of CheatEngine.SDK (Directory.Packages.props);
    - the VersionOverride range of the three SDK-facing libraries (Abstractions, Core, Hosting), which becomes the
      dependency range of their packages;
    - the default SDK version of the Coexistence fixtures;
    - the SDK version stamped into the packed ceplugin template;
    - the CHEATENGINECLIENT9016/9017 guards in Directory.Build.targets.
    The reviewed identity of the pinned package (content hash, signed-file hashes, bridge) is hardcoded next to its
    consumers: tests/CheatEngine.Client.Tests/Packaging/PackagedClientFeedFixture.cs (_pinnedSdkIdentity) and
    tests/CheatEngine.Client.Repository.Tests/LockFiles/LockFileTests.cs (ConsumedSdkContentHash). Tests keep this
    file, those literals, the lock files and the documentation in agreement.

    The properties are assigned unconditionally: an environment variable cannot change the pin, only an explicit
    -p: global property can.

    Bump procedure (no script; every step lands in one pull request): update CheatEngineSdkVersion here; update the
    reviewed identity literals named above to the new package's hashes; regenerate every packages.lock.json with a
    force-evaluate restore of each project (Coexistence fixtures first, one by one, never the solution with a
    force-evaluate restore: that once rewrote their legacy lock files with CentralTransitive entries and broke every
    locked restore); update the SDK version named in prose (SdkPinTests.ProseMentionsOfTheConsumedSdkEqualThePin lists
    the files). Never edit the version by hand without the identity literals, and never move to another major in a
    dependency bump.

    Canary switch (used only by the SDK-side advisory client-canary job): pass
      -p:CheatEngineSdkVersion=<2.x prerelease> -p:CheatEngineSdkUpperBound=3.0.0 -p:CheatEngineSdkCanary=true
      -p:CheatEngineClientAllowUnsupportedSdk=true
    CheatEngineSdkCanary=true turns the CHEATENGINECLIENT9016 build errors into messages so the build can report what
    breaks; packing stays refused. CheatEngineClientAllowUnsupportedSdk=true keeps the consumer check CECLIENT017 a
    warning in the coexistence fixtures, which import the Hosting build targets. Lock files stay enabled: the canary
    restores without locked mode, so NuGet rewrites the lock files of its throw-away checkout, which are never
    committed (disabling them instead fails with NU1005, because every project has a committed lock file).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Consumed CheatEngine.SDK">
    <CheatEngineSdkVersion>1.0.0</CheatEngineSdkVersion>
    <CheatEngineSdkUpperBound>2.0.0</CheatEngineSdkUpperBound>
    <CheatEngineSdkVersionRange>[$(CheatEngineSdkVersion),$(CheatEngineSdkUpperBound))</CheatEngineSdkVersionRange>
    <!-- Raising this is the SDK 2.0 migration, a deliberate maintainer decision; never raise it in a dependency bump. -->
    <_CheatEngineClientSupportedSdkMajor>1</_CheatEngineClientSupportedSdkMajor>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
